(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"You are explaining a 49-year-old woman's melanoma report to her; it gives Breslow thickness, ulceration status and mitotic rate. Which of these does the deck call the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["None of them; the anatomic site of the primary is the dominant prognostic variable","Breslow thickness, with ulceration and mitotic activity further modifying stage-based prognosis","Ulceration, with Breslow thickness and mitotic activity further modifying prognosis","Mitotic rate, with Breslow thickness and ulceration further modifying prognosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why it must be measured accurately at the initial biopsy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 30-year-old Black patient asks whether she needs to worry about melanoma. Which is the most accurate response using the deck's figures?","choices":["Lifetime risk is higher in persons of colour, at about 2% against 0.1 to 0.5% in white individuals","Lifetime risk is lower but not zero — about 0.1 to 0.5% in persons of colour against about 2% in white individuals","Lifetime risk is effectively zero in persons of colour, so routine skin checks are unnecessary","Lifetime risk is the same in all skin tones, at about 2%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — lower risk, and acral and nail sites still need checking. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A melanoma is excised and reported at 1.3 mm Breslow thickness with no ulceration. What does the deck say about sentinel lymph node biopsy here, and what should the patient be told it achieves?","choices":["It should not be offered, since the threshold is 4.0 mm and this lesion is far below it","It should not be offered, since sentinel node biopsy is reserved for patients with palpable nodes","It should be offered or discussed, since the threshold is 1.0 mm; it is a staging procedure and may not itself improve overall survival","It should be offered or discussed, since the threshold is 1.0 mm; it is a therapeutic procedure that reliably improves overall survival"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an honest expectation to set. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 74-year-old farmer has eleven actinic keratoses across his forehead and temples. Which approach does the deck favour, and why?","choices":["Observation with annual review, because eleven lesions in one region indicates indolent disease","Immediate wide excision of the whole affected field, because eleven lesions cannot be treated topically","Field-directed therapy, because multiple lesions in one anatomic region reflect field cancerization risk","Lesion-directed cryotherapy to each of the eleven in turn, because each lesion carries its own independent risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — options are fluorouracil, imiquimod, photodynamic therapy, or fluorouracil plus calcipotriene. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient with a confirmed nail unit melanoma asks whether she will lose the finger. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Amputation is the automatic first treatment for any nail unit melanoma, whatever the depth or extent","Amputation is never performed for nail unit melanoma at any depth or extent","Amputation is decided by the number of digits involved rather than by depth or bone involvement","Amputation is not automatic — digit-sparing wide excision or Mohs with immunostaining is contemporary care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — amputation is reserved for deep, extensive or bone-involving disease, and the digit is preserved where margins can be reliably assessed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man of Mediterranean origin has slowly progressive purple plaques on both lower legs over several years. He is not immunosuppressed. Which form of Kaposi sarcoma is this?","choices":["Epidemic — occurring in acquired immunodeficiency and treated first with antiretroviral therapy","Classic — older men, chronic course, rarely fatal, related to age-associated immune senescence","Endemic — often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa and sometimes rapidly fatal","Iatrogenic — occurring with immunosuppressive therapy and improving when doses are reduced"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the chronic indolent form. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 63-year-old's melanoma is reported at 1.8 mm Breslow thickness. Which re-excision margin applies, and what else does that thickness trigger?","choices":["A 0.5 to 1 cm margin, and routine dermatology follow-up without expert-centre referral","A 1 to 2 cm margin, with referral reserved until nodal disease is demonstrated","A 1 to 2 cm margin, and referral to an expert centre because the lesion is deeper than 1 mm","A 1 cm margin, and referral to an expert centre because the lesion is deeper than 1 mm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same threshold governs both. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 77-year-old has a recurrent basal cell carcinoma at the inner canthus, and the biopsy reports infiltrative histology. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Standard excision with 4 mm margins, since infiltrative histology behaves much like ordinary nodular disease","Topical imiquimod five nights weekly, since periocular skin tolerates surgical excision poorly","Hedgehog pathway inhibitor therapy with vismodegib, since recurrence at this site indicates advanced disease","Mohs micrographic surgery — the canthus is a high-risk site, the tumour is recurrent, and the histology is aggressive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three separate Mohs indications are met. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 48-year-old has a red, eroded, mass-like lesion under one fingernail with no pigment at all. It has progressed over five months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat empirically for onychomycosis and review in six months","Treat as a chronic paronychia with oral antibiotics and review in six weeks","Consider biopsy — amelanotic nail melanoma can look red, pink, eroded or mass-like","Reassure, because the complete absence of a pigmented band reliably excludes melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the absence of pigment does not exclude melanoma, so biopsy any unexplained, progressive single-nail lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has a brown band running the length of the left thumbnail. Over eight months it has widened, the lines within it vary in colour and spacing, and pigment now extends onto the skin of the proximal nail fold. Which finding is most concerning, and what is it called?","choices":["The pigment extending onto the proximal nail fold — the Hutchinson sign, highly concerning for nail unit melanoma","The widening of the band — the Hutchinson sign, highly concerning for nail unit melanoma","The variation in line colour and spacing — the Hutchinson sign, concerning for a benign melanonychia","The eight-month duration — the Hutchinson sign, concerning for a subungual haematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it should prompt urgent expert evaluation regardless of other features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 70-year-old has a biopsy-proven invasive squamous cell carcinoma 1.4 cm across on the temple. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Mohs micrographic surgery, since the temple is a high-risk site and the lesion exceeds 1 cm on the face","Imiquimod or topical fluorouracil, since topical therapy is appropriate for invasive disease at this size","Programmed death 1 blockade, since a lesion over 1 cm is by definition advanced disease","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy, since the temple tolerates destruction well cosmetically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — two separate Mohs indications are met. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient with early patch-stage cutaneous T-cell lymphoma is to begin treatment. Which initial skin-directed options does the deck list?","choices":["Liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel and radiation","Topical corticosteroids, topical mechlorethamine, bexarotene gel","Methotrexate, extracorporeal photopheresis, systemic bexarotene and romidepsin","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil, photodynamic therapy and cryotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four skin-directed options for early disease. Ultraviolet phototherapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 58-year-old kidney transplant recipient, six years post-transplant, has developed his fourth squamous cell carcinoma this year. Which does the deck say about this pattern, and what can be offered to reduce new tumours?","choices":["Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant; nicotinamide reduces new tumours by about 20%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant and are often aggressive; nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge within six weeks of transplant and are usually indolent; nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily reduces new tumours by about 30%","Multiple tumours typically emerge about five years after transplant; oral acitretin reduces new squamous cell carcinoma by about 30%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both the timing and the chemoprevention figure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has a chronic pruritic eruption that has not settled on treatment. Which two clinical clues should raise suspicion of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Annular plaques with central clearing, and a positive potassium hydroxide preparation from the border","Itch out of proportion to the apparent inflammatory activity, and follicular involvement with hair loss","Complete absence of itch, and sparing of the hair follicles throughout the eruption","Rapid complete response to a topical corticosteroid, and sharply demarcated silvery scale"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — folliculotropism discriminates from routine eczema or psoriasis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A patient with an advanced basal cell carcinoma is no longer a surgical candidate. Which systemic option does the deck give?","choices":["A hedgehog pathway inhibitor","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel","Romidepsin or vorinostat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reserved for advanced or metastatic disease or extensive tumour burden. Vismodegib or sonidegib. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 59-year-old has an ivory-white, scar-like plaque on the cheek. He has had no injury there. On careful inspection the pink edge seems to extend further than the visible lesion. Which subtype is this, and what is the concern?","choices":["Pigmented basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Morpheaform or sclerosing basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Superficial basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Nodular basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it dangerous. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient with treated actinic keratoses asks whether he still needs follow-up now the lesions have gone. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Lesions always involute spontaneously, so neither treatment nor surveillance was ever necessary","Surveillance is needed only if a lesion recurs at exactly the same site","Treatment reduces lesion burden but the surrounding field remains at risk","Treatment eradicates the field as well as the lesions, so no further surveillance is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — treating lesions does not treat the field. Ongoing surveillance is still necessary. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"An 82-year-old with classic Kaposi sarcoma has a few troublesome plaques on one shin and no visceral disease. Which local options does the deck give?","choices":["Palliative local therapy — intralesional vincristine, vinblastine or bleomycin, or radiation","Antiretroviral therapy first, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment","Hedgehog pathway inhibitors, vismodegib or sonidegib","Total-skin electron-beam treatment as the initial approach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy and intralesional vinblastine are also listed for all forms. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a nail unit squamous cell carcinoma confirmed on biopsy. Which surgical approach does the deck prefer, and when is amputation considered?","choices":["Complete margin-controlled surgery — Mohs or wide surgical excision","Partial or limited destructive treatment such as curettage or cryotherapy; amputation is reserved for bone invasion","Digital amputation as the automatic first treatment for any nail unit carcinoma, whatever the depth or extent","Topical fluorouracil to the nail fold and bed; amputation is never indicated at any depth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — amputation is reserved for bone invasion or disease that cannot otherwise be cleared, and partial or limited destructive treatment carries a higher recurrence risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"That same patient is not yet on antiretroviral therapy. Which is the first priority in his Kaposi sarcoma management?","choices":["Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel, since systemic chemotherapy is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Begin intralesional vinblastine to each plaque, since local palliation is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Reduce his immunosuppressive medication doses, since dose reduction is the cornerstone of epidemic disease","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — antiretroviral therapy comes before local or systemic cancer treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 66-year-old has a reddish, shiny, scaly thin plaque on the upper back with a thready pearly border and spotty pigment at the edge. Which subtype is this?","choices":["Morpheaform basal cell carcinoma","Pigmented basal cell carcinoma","Superficial basal cell carcinoma","Nodular basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — back and chest are its typical sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient asks why the pathologist's measurement of thickness matters so much. Which is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["Breslow thickness is the dominant prognostic variable and must be measured accurately at the initial biopsy","Breslow thickness determines the cosmetic result of re-excision but has no bearing on prognosis","Breslow thickness is used only to decide the follow-up interval, not the margin or the staging","Breslow thickness matters only when nodes are already positive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why the initial biopsy technique matters. Ulceration and mitotic activity modifying the stage-based prognosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with fair skin has a small red, conical, hard nodule on his lower lip that has ulcerated over two months. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis","Keratoacanthoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and the lip is one of the named high-risk sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Two patients present the same morning. One has had intense blistering sunburns on holidays but works indoors; the other has worked outdoors for forty years with steady daily exposure. Which pattern favours which diagnosis?","choices":["Intense intermittent exposure favours basal cell carcinoma; prolonged cumulative exposure favours squamous cell carcinoma","Intense intermittent exposure favours squamous cell carcinoma; prolonged cumulative exposure favours basal cell carcinoma","Both patterns favour basal cell carcinoma equally, since ultraviolet exposure is not subdivided in this way","Both patterns favour melanoma, since keratinocyte carcinoma is not ultraviolet-driven"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the exposure pattern is a genuine discriminator. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 78-year-old has a slowly enlarging irregular brown patch on the cheek, present for years, on skin that is heavily sun-damaged. Which melanoma subtype does this fit?","choices":["Superficial spreading","Nodular","Acral lentiginous","Lentigo maligna"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults, slow radial growth. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A patient with early-stage cutaneous T-cell lymphoma asks whether aggressive chemotherapy now would cure it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Early aggressive systemic chemotherapy reliably cures the disease and prevents progression, so it should begin immediately","No treatment of any kind alters the course, so observation alone is appropriate at every stage","Total-skin electron-beam treatment should be given first to every patient regardless of stage","Early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure the disease or prevent progression, and may itself cause harm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — overly aggressive therapy may cause complications and premature death, so a stage-directed, skin-first approach suits most early disease. Aggression is a harm here rather than a virtue. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 45-year-old patient has a suspicious pigmented lesion that needs sampling. Which is the initial diagnostic test the deck names?","choices":["Dermoscopy alone, with tissue reserved for lesions that later change","Sentinel lymph node biopsy as the first tissue step","Whole-body imaging before any tissue is taken","Biopsy or excision to confirm the diagnosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — tissue is required before staging decisions. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A patient presents with a lesion you have not seen before. Before you commit to a diagnosis, what does the deck ask you to do?","choices":["Characterise it systematically","Name the most likely diagnosis, then look only for the features that support it","Photograph it and wait for the dermatology opinion before describing anything","Biopsy immediately, since description adds nothing once tissue is available"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. Lesion type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and evolution. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 69-year-old has an ulcer on the ear that has not healed in three months, with a firm indurated base. Which diagnosis should be excluded first, and which feature makes the site significant?","choices":["Actinic keratosis — the ear is one of the named high-risk sites for progression","Kaposi sarcoma — the ear is one of the named high-risk sites for visceral spread","Squamous cell carcinoma — the ear is one of the named high-risk sites for recurrence and metastasis","Basal cell carcinoma — the ear is one of the named high-risk sites for recurrence and metastasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a non-healing ulcer is one of its presentations. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old has had itchy scaly plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm. They have been treated as eczema and then as psoriasis without lasting response, and he has patchy hair loss within some plaques. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma","Psoriasis","Eczematous dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chronicity, treatment resistance and follicular involvement together. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"}]};
